  <section>
    <h2>5. Tools, automation, and computer use</h2>
    <p>
      Vector can read, create, edit, and delete files; run commands and processes; use a browser; operate
      connected services; and carry out background or scheduled tasks. Depending on enabled features and
      permissions, it can also capture a selected screen or window and control mouse and keyboard input.
      Actions may affect real accounts, shared repositories, infrastructure, or production systems and may
      cause charges or disclose information.
    </p>
    <p>
      You control the instructions, permissions, and integrations you enable. Standing approvals and automated
      workflows can allow actions without asking you again for every step. Grant only authority you possess,
      review permissions and intended destinations, and supervise sensitive work. Keep independent backups
      and verify changes before publishing, merging, or deploying. Permission prompts, checkpoints, and
      protective controls reduce risk but cannot guarantee that every unintended action will be prevented or
      reversible. Stop computer use and take over when private information or a sensitive action is involved.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>6. Providers, plugins, and external runtimes</h2>
    <p>
      Models, MCP servers, plugins, browser destinations, cloud providers, and external coding runtimes are
      separate services. This includes the model providers you connect and runtimes such as Claude Code,
      Codex CLI, and Cursor Agent. Their terms, privacy practices, permissions, availability, and charges
      apply to your use of them. Using Vector does not grant rights to their paid services or imply endorsement
      or affiliation.
    </p>
    <p>
      Review the provider and account used by each task. A connected tool or runtime may transmit repository
      context, keep its own records, or perform actions under its own permission settings. You are responsible
      for your agreements with those providers. We do not control their independent changes or outages.
    </p>
    <p id="free-models">
      Free models inside of Vector are offered by OpenRouter and the providers that serve them, under their terms,
      which can limit use to adults, to tools such as coding agents, or to content that is not confidential. Some of
      those providers may log prompts and use them to train their models. Vector labels such models “May train on
      your prompts” and, from desktop 1.99.109 and the next CLI release, asks you to accept that once before sending
      your first prompt to one. Vector leaves out free models whose terms bar this use, such as NVIDIA's trial
      endpoints. Vector itself never trains on your prompts.
    </p>
    <p id="codium-data">
      Vector Codium requests go from Vector through Vector's gateway and OpenRouter to the provider serving the model
      you choose. Each request asks OpenRouter to use only endpoints it records as zero data retention that do not
      collect request data. This is a routing restriction that relies on OpenRouter's classification and each
      provider's published policy; it is not a guarantee by Vector or an independent audit, and Vector does not
      control what OpenRouter or a provider does with a request. Zero data retention has exceptions: OpenRouter
      screens requests for child sexual abuse material and may keep and report flagged content as the law requires,
      providers may hold part of a request in a short-lived prompt cache or for their own automated safety checks,
      and OpenRouter keeps request metadata such as token counts. If no qualifying endpoint is available, the request
      fails rather than going to an endpoint that keeps data. The <a href="/legal/privacy#codium">Privacy Policy</a>
      describes these details.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>7. Publishing and cloud resources</h2>
    <p>
      Publishing can upload files, build artifacts, configuration, or other selected content to a hosting
      provider and make it publicly accessible. Check the content and access settings before publishing; do
      not upload secrets or material you lack permission to disclose. You are responsible for the content
      you publish and for cloud resources and charges you authorize. Deleting your Vector account or
      uninstalling Vector does not automatically delete deployments or cancel separate provider subscriptions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Where Vector-hosted public session sharing is available, publishing requires your explicit consent and
      makes the visible transcript accessible to anyone with the link. You choose whether later conversation
      updates are public; separately remembered consent can authorize automatic publication of future sessions
      when automatic sharing is enabled. Review the transcript and disclose only material you have permission
      to make public. The link expires within 30 days, and successful unsharing or account deletion removes
